{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The derived rule is internal to EA: if EA proves φ, then EA proves Kφ. Target requires only that 𝓢 prove Kφ. Even if 𝓢 extends 𝓑, Kφ could be provable only in the extension. Thus the two-system condition is a consequence of the internal rule, not the full rule itself."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Nothing in Target requires that 𝓢 include 𝓑. Inclusion would require, at minimum, that every sentence provable in 𝓑 also be provable in 𝓢. Requiring 𝓢 to prove Kφ when 𝓑 proves φ does not ensure that 𝓢 proves φ."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "As acknowledged, the predicate works with abstract sentences and provability relations. It does not identify 𝓑 as classical first-order EA or represent the source’s use of universal closure for formulas with free variables."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Target has the correct metatheoretic direction and captures the derived rule when both systems are instantiated as EA. For distinct systems it states only a transfer of provability from φ to Kφ, without requiring inclusion or knowledge provability in the base itself."
}
